### Sign-in, sessions and the users routes. Run the requests top to bottom.
### The API sets three cookies (op_access, op_refresh, op_csrf). REST Client
### keeps them for you, but it does not copy op_csrf into a header: the
### @csrf variable below does, once you paste the cookie's value into it.
@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@csrf = paste the op_csrf cookie value here after signing in
@demoPassword = orderpilot-demo-password

### What the sign-in page offers. Public. With DEMO_MODE=false, demoRoles is empty.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/options

### Demo sign-in as one of the nine roles. Only with DEMO_MODE=true: otherwise this
### route does not exist and answers 404. Roles: SYSTEM_ADMIN, PRODUCT_MANAGER,
### APPROVER, SUPPORT, WAREHOUSE, FINANCE, LOGISTICS, MANAGEMENT, AUDITOR.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/demo-login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "role": "SYSTEM_ADMIN" }

### Sign in with the seeded email and the DEMO_PASSWORD from your .env.
### An unknown email and a wrong password get the same answer (INVALID_CREDENTIALS).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "system.admin@example.test", "password": "{{demoPassword}}" }

### Validation: a bad email is refused with VALIDATION_FAILED and a field list.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "not-an-email", "password": "x" }

### Who am I, what may I do, and when does the access token end.
### Without a session: 401 UNAUTHENTICATED.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/me

### Exchange the refresh cookie for a new pair. Each refresh token works once:
### sending an old one again revokes the whole sign-in (REFRESH_REUSED).
### Needs the CSRF header; without it: 403 CSRF_INVALID.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

### Sign out. Needs the CSRF header too.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/logout
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

### Two-step sign-in. A person who turned on two-step verification gets no session
### from the login request above: it answers 401 TWO_STEP_REQUIRED and sets a short
### cookie (op_two_step, five minutes, sent by the client to the verify URL only).
### Finish with a six-digit code from the authenticator app, or one recovery code.
### The client sends the challenge cookie for you. Exactly one of code and
### recoveryCode. Errors: 422 TWO_STEP_CODE_INVALID (the first four wrong codes),
### 401 TWO_STEP_CHALLENGE_ENDED (no challenge, expired, or the fifth wrong code:
### sign in again), 429 TWO_STEP_LOCKED (wait the Retry-After seconds, or use a
### recovery code). No CSRF header: like login it has no CSRF cookie yet, so it
### takes only JSON from our own origin.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step/verify
Content-Type: application/json

{ "code": "123456" }

### The same step with a recovery code. Each one works once.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step/verify
Content-Type: application/json

{ "recoveryCode": "ABCDE-FGHJK-LMNPQ" }

### Two-step settings. Signed in, with the CSRF header. A secret (the setup key, the
### recovery codes) is only ever in the body of an answer, with Cache-Control: no-store.
### Is two-step on? hasPassword false means there is no password sign-in to guard.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step

### Step 1 of turning it on: a new secret and its setup key (also as an otpauth
### address for a QR code). A password session sends its password; a demo session
### sends none. Errors: 400 no password, 422 PASSWORD_INCORRECT, 409 CONFLICT (already
### on, the account has no password, or a demo session on an account that can reset
### other people's two-step).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "password": "{{demoPassword}}" }

### Step 2: the first six-digit code from the app switches it on and shows ten
### recovery codes, once. Errors: 422 TWO_STEP_CODE_INVALID, 409 TWO_STEP_SETUP_ENDED
### (nothing pending, or ten minutes passed), 429 TWO_STEP_LOCKED (see Retry-After).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step/enable
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "code": "123456" }

### Turn it off: the password (password session) and a current code or a recovery
### code, exactly one of the two. A session cookie alone cannot do this.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step/disable
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "password": "{{demoPassword}}", "recoveryCode": "ABCDE-FGHJK-LMNPQ" }

### Ten new recovery codes. Needs a current code (a recovery code is not accepted
### here). The old codes stop working.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/two-step/recovery-codes
Content-Type: application/json
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

{ "code": "123456" }

### Users. Reading needs users.read, changing needs users.manage (SYSTEM_ADMIN
### holds both). Another role gets 403 FORBIDDEN, and the refusal is audited.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users?page=1&pageSize=25

### Only the active users, as the System Admin's home tile counts them.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users?active=true&pageSize=1

### One role, and words in the name or the email, ignoring case (P-45).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users?role=SUPPORT&search=tan

### One user. Replace the id with one from the list.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000000

### Disable a user (never deleted). Their sessions end at their next request.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000000/disable
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

### Enable them again.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000000/enable
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}

### Reset another person's two-step verification (users.manage, SYSTEM_ADMIN holds it),
### for someone who lost the phone and the recovery codes. It removes the second
### step, ends ALL of that person's sessions (demo ones too) and is audited as
### user.two-step-reset. Answers wasOn and sessionsEnded. A person with no two-step
### is a harmless no-op. Never your own: 409 CONFLICT, and the refusal is audited.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000000/two-step/reset
X-CSRF-Token: {{csrf}}
